content.theory:
The `fetch()` function is the modern way to talk to servers. A **REST API** is a set of URLs that return data — usually JSON. `fetch(url)` sends a request and returns a Promise that resolves to a `Response` object, which you must parse before you can use the data.

### Promise style
```js
fetch("https://api.example.com/user")
  .then((res) => res.json())
  .then((data) => console.log(data.name));
```
`.json()` is async too — it reads the body and parses it, so it returns a promise you must also resolve. This is a classic two-step: get the response, then get the data.

### async / await style
```js
async function load() {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/user");
  const data = await res.json();
  console.log(data.name);
}
```
Cleaner and easier to read. Both styles are used in real codebases, so know both.

### Handling errors properly
`fetch` only rejects on network failures — a 404 or 500 still resolves! Always check `response.ok`:
```js
const res = await fetch(url);
if (!res.ok) {
  throw new Error("Request failed: " + res.status);
}
const data = await res.json();
```

### Loading / error state pattern
```js
let status = "loading";
try {
  const data = await load();
  status = "loaded";
} catch (err) {
  status = "error: " + err.message;
}
```
Real apps flip between three states — loading, loaded, error — and re-render the UI on each change. Never assume the fetch succeeded.

### JSON.stringify — the reverse
Sending data back uses `JSON.stringify(obj)` to turn an object into a JSON string. The round-trip is: `JSON.stringify` to encode, `JSON.parse` to decode. Remember `response.ok` checks HTTP status, `res.json()` parses the body, and an unawaited fetch is a Promise you never use — a silent, confusing bug.

